1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,859
Session eight.

2
00:00:01,139 --> 00:00:02,855
Streaming video at large scale.

3
00:00:03,135 --> 00:00:05,130
It starts with a live cricket scorecard.

4
00:00:05,410 --> 00:00:09,918
And by the end you will see that every live
stream is a scorecard with bigger bytes.

5
00:00:10,866 --> 00:00:14,098
Look at three requests from the same
app, during the same match.

6
00:00:14,433 --> 00:00:15,308
The scorecard.

7
00:00:15,588 --> 00:00:19,151
A thousand and eighty-eight bytes,
fetched every fifteen seconds.

8
00:00:19,499 --> 00:00:20,546
A video playlist.

9
00:00:20,826 --> 00:00:23,895
Two hundred twenty-eight bytes,
fetched every four seconds.

10
00:00:24,233 --> 00:00:25,396
And a video segment.

11
00:00:25,676 --> 00:00:29,260
Eight hundred twenty-three thousand
bytes, fetched exactly once.

12
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:34,464
It is the same loop each time: poll something
small, then fetch numbered pieces.

13
00:00:34,744 --> 00:00:38,915
Every week of this course shows up in
this one system: one live match,

14
00:00:38,915 --> 00:00:40,345
on fifty million phones.

15
00:00:41,266 --> 00:00:42,323
Here is the brief.

16
00:00:42,666 --> 00:00:45,497
Twenty-five to forty-five seconds between balls.

17
00:00:45,777 --> 00:00:47,672
An over every four or five minutes.

18
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,252
Fifty million concurrent viewers at the peak.

19
00:00:50,599 --> 00:00:53,454
Twenty million on average, over a four-hour match.

20
00:00:53,799 --> 00:00:58,727
The scorecard is four to five kilobytes of
JSON, about one kilobyte gzipped.

21
00:00:59,066 --> 00:01:01,149
And a hundred events like this every year.

22
00:01:02,066 --> 00:01:03,772
There are two families of delivery.

23
00:01:04,052 --> 00:01:06,513
Either the client asks, or the server tells.

24
00:01:06,833 --> 00:01:08,496
On the left, non-persistent.

25
00:01:08,776 --> 00:01:14,749
Polling, conditional GETs that come back three oh
four, and caches, first nginx, then a CDN.

26
00:01:15,029 --> 00:01:18,184
Any server can answer, and nobody
remembers who asked.

27
00:01:18,533 --> 00:01:19,876
On the right, persistent.

28
00:01:20,156 --> 00:01:25,527
Long polling, server-sent events,
WebSockets, publish subscribe with MQTT,

29
00:01:25,527 --> 00:01:28,430
or your own binary protocol on a socket.

30
00:01:28,766 --> 00:01:29,986
That is the whole trade.

31
00:01:30,266 --> 00:01:35,607
Polling is ordinary GETs: stateless servers,
cacheable, a CDN can sit in front.

32
00:01:35,887 --> 00:01:41,024
Push means every viewer holds a connection for
the whole match: stateful servers,

33
00:01:41,024 --> 00:01:42,799
and nothing a CDN can cache.

34
00:01:43,733 --> 00:01:44,712
Polling first.

35
00:01:44,992 --> 00:01:47,318
Done properly, it is about seven lines.

36
00:01:47,666 --> 00:01:48,854
Remember the ETag.

37
00:01:49,134 --> 00:01:50,797
Send it back in If-None-Match.

38
00:01:51,077 --> 00:01:53,205
On a two hundred, render the new card.

39
00:01:53,485 --> 00:01:58,238
On a three oh four, keep what you have: an empty
body, but the request still happens.

40
00:01:58,566 --> 00:02:01,193
Then the jitter: plus or minus twenty percent.

41
00:02:01,473 --> 00:02:04,603
Without it, fifty million phones
poll in the same second.

42
00:02:04,933 --> 00:02:06,161
With it, they spread out.

43
00:02:06,441 --> 00:02:09,193
And the freshness you can promise, at worst,

44
00:02:09,193 --> 00:02:12,572
is one poll interval plus every
cache TTL on the path.

45
00:02:13,499 --> 00:02:14,378
Long polling.

46
00:02:14,658 --> 00:02:17,118
The server holds the GET until there is news.

47
00:02:18,166 --> 00:02:21,706
The phone asks for anything after
version eighteen forty-one.

48
00:02:21,986 --> 00:02:26,099
The server holds the request until ball
eighteen forty-two happens,

49
00:02:26,099 --> 00:02:27,818
or twenty-five seconds pass.

50
00:02:28,166 --> 00:02:31,045
Then it answers, and the phone
asks again immediately.

51
00:02:32,166 --> 00:02:34,148
A held request is a held socket.

52
00:02:34,428 --> 00:02:39,033
In Little's Law, W is now twenty-five
seconds, not thirty milliseconds.

53
00:02:39,366 --> 00:02:41,221
Answer before the idle timeouts.

54
00:02:41,501 --> 00:02:44,688
nginx proxy read timeout and the AWS load

55
00:02:44,688 --> 00:02:48,730
balancer idle timeout both
default to sixty seconds.

56
00:02:49,066 --> 00:02:50,614
And it synchronises the herd.

57
00:02:50,894 --> 00:02:54,189
A wicket completes twenty million
responses in one second,

58
00:02:54,189 --> 00:02:57,257
so twenty million new requests
arrive the next second.

59
00:02:57,599 --> 00:03:01,155
Keep it in mind, because it
comes back standardised:

60
00:03:01,155 --> 00:03:05,189
Low-Latency HLS's blocking playlist
reload is long polling.

61
00:03:06,133 --> 00:03:07,359
Server-Sent Events.

62
00:03:07,639 --> 00:03:09,353
One response that never ends.

63
00:03:09,699 --> 00:03:11,794
The request asks for text/event stream.

64
00:03:12,074 --> 00:03:15,276
The response is a two hundred that
simply never finishes.

65
00:03:15,556 --> 00:03:19,649
Each event has an id, a type, and
data, and ends at a blank line.

66
00:03:19,966 --> 00:03:21,449
That is framing by delimiter.

67
00:03:21,729 --> 00:03:24,512
SMTP's lone dot, in another costume.

68
00:03:24,833 --> 00:03:28,651
To resume, the browser sends Last-
Event-ID when it reconnects,

69
00:03:28,651 --> 00:03:31,317
and the server replays only what was missed.

70
00:03:31,597 --> 00:03:34,029
The retry field sets the reconnect delay.

71
00:03:34,366 --> 00:03:40,151
About six per browser over HTTP one point one,
since each tab holds its own connection.

72
00:03:40,431 --> 00:03:42,635
HTTP two multiplexes them.

73
00:03:42,915 --> 00:03:45,464
And it is one way only, server to client.

74
00:03:46,399 --> 00:03:47,334
WebSockets.

75
00:03:47,614 --> 00:03:51,362
HTTP upgrades itself into a two-way framed pipe.

76
00:03:52,399 --> 00:03:56,850
The browser sends an ordinary GET with
Upgrade websocket and a random key.

77
00:03:57,199 --> 00:04:00,229
The server answers one oh one,
Switching Protocols.

78
00:04:00,509 --> 00:04:05,743
The Accept header is base sixty-four of the
SHA one of the key plus a fixed GUID.

79
00:04:06,023 --> 00:04:08,583
Week one's base sixty-four, showing up again.

80
00:04:08,899 --> 00:04:10,741
After that, everything is frames.

81
00:04:11,021 --> 00:04:15,688
Byte one holds FIN and the opcode:
text, binary, ping, pong, close.

82
00:04:15,968 --> 00:04:18,192
Byte two holds the mask bit and a length.

83
00:04:18,472 --> 00:04:23,940
One twenty-six means a sixteen-bit length follows,
one twenty-seven means sixty-four bits.

84
00:04:24,220 --> 00:04:27,608
Length-prefixed, not delimited:
the other framing answer.

85
00:04:27,933 --> 00:04:31,548
And every frame from the client is
masked with a four-byte key,

86
00:04:31,548 --> 00:04:35,221
so stray bytes can never look like a
request to a caching proxy.

87
00:04:35,501 --> 00:04:37,241
That is a cache poisoning defence.

88
00:04:38,166 --> 00:04:39,201
MQTT.

89
00:04:39,481 --> 00:04:41,853
Publish once, and the broker fans it out.

90
00:04:42,200 --> 00:04:47,051
Phones connect with a keepalive, and subscribe
to a topic: match/four twelve/score.

91
00:04:47,331 --> 00:04:49,109
The publisher sends one message.

92
00:04:49,433 --> 00:04:51,725
The broker copies it to every subscriber.

93
00:04:52,005 --> 00:04:54,083
And because it is a retained message,

94
00:04:54,083 --> 00:04:58,633
the broker keeps the last score and hands it
to every new subscriber immediately.

95
00:04:58,913 --> 00:05:00,657
A cache, inside the broker.

96
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,525
Quality of service one is at least
once, so duplicates happen.

97
00:05:04,805 --> 00:05:08,367
Apply updates by version number and
duplicates are harmless.

98
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:13,316
Keepalives are two bytes each way: C
zero zero, and D zero zero.

99
00:05:13,596 --> 00:05:16,871
They keep the NAT mapping alive, and
wake the phone's radio.

100
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:22,324
And the remaining length is a varint: seven bits
per byte, the top bit says more is coming.

101
00:05:23,266 --> 00:05:28,609
How Hotstar held fifty million sockets, from their
engineering blog in June twenty nineteen.

102
00:05:28,889 --> 00:05:31,496
They chose MQTT with the EMQX broker.

103
00:05:31,833 --> 00:05:37,050
One EMQX node on a c five four x large: two
hundred fifty thousand connections.

104
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:41,274
A cluster, with the publisher split from
the subscribers: two million.

105
00:05:41,554 --> 00:05:44,145
The Mnesia database wants fewer than ten nodes.

106
00:05:44,466 --> 00:05:45,980
Then the load balancer wall.

107
00:05:46,260 --> 00:05:49,592
An ELB drops at about half a
million without pre-warming.

108
00:05:49,872 --> 00:05:52,672
Four million per pre-warmed classic ELB.

109
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,349
Five clusters of eight subscribe nodes,
bridged together: past ten million.

110
00:05:57,666 --> 00:06:02,271
And a reverse bridge: a Go service on each
subscriber pulls from the publisher.

111
00:06:02,551 --> 00:06:04,436
Two hundred nodes, fifty million.

112
00:06:04,766 --> 00:06:05,542
Why push?

113
00:06:05,822 --> 00:06:09,977
Their social feed was different for every
user and tied to live moments.

114
00:06:10,257 --> 00:06:12,478
Caching at any level was not possible.

115
00:06:12,758 --> 00:06:16,823
A scorecard is the opposite: one
file, identical for everyone.

116
00:06:17,766 --> 00:06:20,047
So what does a held socket actually cost?

117
00:06:21,066 --> 00:06:21,847
Machines.

118
00:06:22,127 --> 00:06:24,442
Each phone lives on one box for hours.

119
00:06:24,722 --> 00:06:27,000
A hundred to two hundred thousand per box.

120
00:06:27,280 --> 00:06:32,641
New boxes sit empty until people reconnect, so
rebalancing means disconnecting people.

121
00:06:32,966 --> 00:06:33,594
Memory.

122
00:06:33,874 --> 00:06:37,686
Kernel buffers, TLS state, a
broker process per client.

123
00:06:37,966 --> 00:06:40,781
Two hundred fifty thousand phones on thirty-two

124
00:06:40,781 --> 00:06:44,196
gigabytes is about a hundred
thirty-seven kilobytes each.

125
00:06:44,533 --> 00:06:45,252
Network.

126
00:06:45,532 --> 00:06:48,562
A sixty-second keepalive across
twenty million idle

127
00:06:48,562 --> 00:06:52,186
phones is three hundred thirty-three
thousand pings a second,

128
00:06:52,186 --> 00:06:54,087
before the first ball is bowled.

129
00:06:54,433 --> 00:06:56,827
Load balancers hold every socket too.

130
00:06:57,166 --> 00:06:57,954
And testing.

131
00:06:58,234 --> 00:07:00,872
One client IP has twenty-eight thousand two

132
00:07:00,872 --> 00:07:04,000
hundred thirty-two ephemeral
ports per destination.

133
00:07:04,280 --> 00:07:06,062
Week one's sixteen-bit ports.

134
00:07:06,342 --> 00:07:10,606
So two hundred fifty thousand test sockets
need at least nine source IPs.

135
00:07:11,533 --> 00:07:12,693
Little's Law, twice.

136
00:07:12,973 --> 00:07:14,914
L equals lambda times W.

137
00:07:15,233 --> 00:07:19,617
Polling through a CDN: one point six
seven million requests a second,

138
00:07:19,617 --> 00:07:22,921
each spending about thirty
milliseconds at the edge.

139
00:07:23,201 --> 00:07:24,620
Fifty thousand in flight.

140
00:07:24,966 --> 00:07:27,409
Push: W is the whole match, four hours.

141
00:07:27,689 --> 00:07:29,230
Fifty million sockets held.

142
00:07:29,566 --> 00:07:32,657
A thousand times the concurrency
for the same audience.

143
00:07:32,937 --> 00:07:35,802
Only because W went from milliseconds to hours.

144
00:07:36,733 --> 00:07:39,370
Do the arithmetic before you
draw the architecture.

145
00:07:39,650 --> 00:07:41,298
Polling every thirty seconds.

146
00:07:41,633 --> 00:07:46,853
Twenty million viewers, fourteen thousand four
hundred seconds, one request every thirty.

147
00:07:47,133 --> 00:07:49,349
Nine point six billion requests a match.

148
00:07:49,666 --> 00:07:52,170
Peak rate: fifty million over thirty seconds.

149
00:07:52,450 --> 00:07:55,332
One point six seven million requests a second.

150
00:07:55,666 --> 00:07:57,998
Bytes: nine point six terabytes a match.

151
00:07:58,333 --> 00:08:01,163
Poll every fifteen seconds and everything doubles.

152
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:05,431
Times a hundred events: nine hundred
sixty billion requests a year.

153
00:08:06,366 --> 00:08:09,948
Four ways to pay for one match,
at AWS list prices.

154
00:08:10,266 --> 00:08:16,190
S3 straight to phones: about four point seven
thousand dollars, mostly GET fees.

155
00:08:16,470 --> 00:08:18,578
It breaks first on the request rate limit.

156
00:08:18,900 --> 00:08:24,714
An nginx fleet behind a load balancer: twelve to
eighteen hundred dollars, mostly egress.

157
00:08:24,994 --> 00:08:29,906
It breaks on twenty to fifty million idle keep-
alive sockets at the load balancer.

158
00:08:30,233 --> 00:08:33,274
Through a CDN: twelve and a half thousand dollars.

159
00:08:33,554 --> 00:08:36,017
Ninety-two percent of it is request fees.

160
00:08:36,297 --> 00:08:37,335
Nothing breaks.

161
00:08:37,615 --> 00:08:38,744
It is just the bill.

162
00:08:39,066 --> 00:08:43,191
And push on two hundred servers: nine
hundred to sixteen hundred dollars.

163
00:08:43,471 --> 00:08:45,996
It breaks on reconnect storms and rebalancing.

164
00:08:46,333 --> 00:08:50,870
So at list price, push is the cheapest per
match, and the CDN is the dearest.

165
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:55,319
And S3 runs out of requests before
it runs out of money.

166
00:08:55,666 --> 00:08:59,949
S3 promises at least fifty-five
hundred GETs a second per prefix.

167
00:09:00,266 --> 00:09:05,339
Fifty million phones polling every thirty seconds
ask for one point six seven million.

168
00:09:05,619 --> 00:09:07,623
Three hundred and three times over.

169
00:09:07,966 --> 00:09:12,084
The documentation says: you may see some
five oh three Slow Down errors.

170
00:09:12,364 --> 00:09:17,222
Fifty million clients retrying without
backoff is session seven's retry storm,

171
00:09:17,222 --> 00:09:18,779
aimed at your own bucket.

172
00:09:19,700 --> 00:09:21,914
Why is the CDN so expensive here?

173
00:09:22,194 --> 00:09:24,546
Because you are billed per request and per byte.

174
00:09:25,666 --> 00:09:32,637
CloudFront in India charges one dollar twenty per
million HTTPS requests, whatever the size.

175
00:09:32,966 --> 00:09:34,837
And about eleven cents per gigabyte.

176
00:09:35,117 --> 00:09:37,501
That line grows with the size of the object.

177
00:09:37,833 --> 00:09:40,043
They cross at about eleven kilobytes.

178
00:09:40,323 --> 00:09:43,785
Below it you pay per request: the
one-kilobyte scorecard,

179
00:09:43,785 --> 00:09:46,397
the two hundred twenty-eight byte playlist.

180
00:09:46,677 --> 00:09:50,097
Ninety-two percent of the
scorecard's bill is request fees.

181
00:09:50,377 --> 00:09:52,851
Above it you pay per byte: video segments.

182
00:09:53,200 --> 00:09:54,910
So why prefer polling anyway?

183
00:09:55,190 --> 00:09:58,679
It is cacheable, one file
identical for fifty million.

184
00:09:58,959 --> 00:10:01,029
It is stateless, any edge can answer.

185
00:10:01,309 --> 00:10:02,850
And it is already paid for:

186
00:10:02,850 --> 00:10:06,960
the scorecard is zero point two eight
percent of the match's video bill.

187
00:10:07,240 --> 00:10:10,418
And, as you will see, it is
exactly how video works.

188
00:10:11,366 --> 00:10:13,069
Now, making polling survive.

189
00:10:13,349 --> 00:10:14,912
First, the conditional GET.

190
00:10:15,933 --> 00:10:18,507
The ETag is the scorecard's version number.

191
00:10:18,787 --> 00:10:20,361
A fingerprint, not a clock.

192
00:10:20,641 --> 00:10:23,315
If-Modified-Since only resolves to the second.

193
00:10:23,633 --> 00:10:28,085
Nothing changed, so three oh four, with
Cache-Control public, max age ten.

194
00:10:28,433 --> 00:10:31,114
It saves the body, the parse and the re-render.

195
00:10:31,394 --> 00:10:34,847
It does not save the request fee, the
round trip, or the headers.

196
00:10:35,166 --> 00:10:37,959
Where it pays most is between CDN tiers.

197
00:10:38,239 --> 00:10:43,205
The edge, the mid-tier and the origin exchange
three oh fours instead of copies.

198
00:10:44,133 --> 00:10:46,404
Here is a trap: TTLs add up.

199
00:10:46,733 --> 00:10:50,956
Thirty seconds at the mid-tier, thirty at
the edge, and a thirty-second poll.

200
00:10:51,236 --> 00:10:52,737
Up to ninety seconds stale.

201
00:10:53,017 --> 00:10:54,506
Two or three balls behind.

202
00:10:54,833 --> 00:11:01,382
CDN TTLs of five and five, with a fifteen-
second poll: up to twenty-five seconds.

203
00:11:01,662 --> 00:11:02,803
Inside one ball.

204
00:11:03,133 --> 00:11:07,985
Worst-case staleness is the poll interval
plus every cache TTL on the path.

205
00:11:08,333 --> 00:11:14,370
So keep each CDN TTL at most half of what you
promise, and refresh before expiry.

206
00:11:14,650 --> 00:11:19,965
Akamai calls it prefresh: refetch at twenty-
five seconds of a thirty-second TTL.

207
00:11:20,900 --> 00:11:24,470
When a popular object expires,
everyone misses at once.

208
00:11:24,750 --> 00:11:25,859
The thundering herd.

209
00:11:26,200 --> 00:11:31,605
Twenty thousand phones ask one edge for the same
object in the same fifty milliseconds.

210
00:11:31,933 --> 00:11:37,192
With request collapsing, the edge sends one
request upstream, and the origin sees one.

211
00:11:37,472 --> 00:11:38,359
The rest queue.

212
00:11:38,699 --> 00:11:39,603
Four fixes.

213
00:11:39,883 --> 00:11:42,611
One, request collapsing: proxy cache lock.

214
00:11:42,891 --> 00:11:46,811
Two, serve stale while updating,
RFC fifty-eight sixty-one.

215
00:11:47,091 --> 00:11:48,956
Three, prefetch before expiry.

216
00:11:49,236 --> 00:11:50,875
Four, jitter from the client.

217
00:11:51,199 --> 00:11:53,203
But collapsing needs a shareable answer.

218
00:11:53,483 --> 00:11:56,470
Cache-Control public releases
every waiter at once.

219
00:11:56,750 --> 00:12:00,764
A private answer, or a stray Set-
Cookie, dequeues them one by one,

220
00:12:00,764 --> 00:12:04,659
and Fastly then skips collapsing for
that object for two minutes.

221
00:12:04,939 --> 00:12:09,587
Also check defaults: Cloudflare does not
cache dot json out of the box,

222
00:12:09,587 --> 00:12:11,289
so every poll reaches you.

223
00:12:12,233 --> 00:12:13,344
Negative caching.

224
00:12:13,624 --> 00:12:14,780
Should you cache errors?

225
00:12:15,099 --> 00:12:17,007
Five hundreds: yes, briefly.

226
00:12:17,287 --> 00:12:19,945
One or two seconds of a cached five oh three

227
00:12:19,945 --> 00:12:23,267
shields a struggling origin from
fifty million retries.

228
00:12:23,547 --> 00:12:25,822
Each edge sends one probe per TTL.

229
00:12:26,166 --> 00:12:28,028
Four oh fours: almost never.

230
00:12:28,308 --> 00:12:32,931
A four oh four for a file that is about to
exist, like the newest live segment,

231
00:12:32,931 --> 00:12:35,389
keeps being served after the file appears.

232
00:12:35,669 --> 00:12:39,205
Cloudflare's default four oh four
TTL is three minutes.

233
00:12:39,533 --> 00:12:40,084
The rule:

234
00:12:40,084 --> 00:12:45,165
a negative TTL must be far shorter than the
time the missing thing takes to appear.

235
00:12:46,099 --> 00:12:47,861
Send diffs, not the whole card.

236
00:12:48,199 --> 00:12:51,254
The full card is four thousand one
hundred seventeen bytes.

237
00:12:51,599 --> 00:12:53,641
Gzipped, a thousand and eighty-eight.

238
00:12:53,966 --> 00:12:57,881
One ball, as a gzipped JSON Patch: one
hundred eighty-three bytes.

239
00:12:58,199 --> 00:13:01,734
With push, the patch rides on SSE or MQTT.

240
00:13:02,014 --> 00:13:06,172
A phone that sees a gap in version
numbers fetches the full card once.

241
00:13:06,499 --> 00:13:11,829
With pull, a since parameter gives every client
its own URL, and the hit rate collapses.

242
00:13:12,109 --> 00:13:16,220
Instead, publish one file with the
version and the last few changes.

243
00:13:17,166 --> 00:13:18,666
And now look what we have built.

244
00:13:19,699 --> 00:13:21,965
The version number is the media sequence.

245
00:13:22,299 --> 00:13:24,906
The recent changes are the last few segments.

246
00:13:25,233 --> 00:13:27,698
The poll interval is the target duration.

247
00:13:28,033 --> 00:13:32,720
A small index that everyone polls, pointing at
numbered pieces that never change.

248
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:36,142
That design has a name: HTTP Live Streaming.

249
00:13:36,422 --> 00:13:37,277
HLS.

250
00:13:38,199 --> 00:13:39,776
HLS in one picture.

251
00:13:40,056 --> 00:13:41,313
Control, and data.

252
00:13:41,633 --> 00:13:43,509
The master playlist, fetched once.

253
00:13:43,789 --> 00:13:47,245
A menu of renditions: bandwidth,
resolution, codecs.

254
00:13:47,525 --> 00:13:48,544
That is control.

255
00:13:48,866 --> 00:13:54,434
The media playlist for the chosen rendition, re-
fetched every target duration, four seconds.

256
00:13:54,714 --> 00:13:56,320
It lists the last ten segments.

257
00:13:56,600 --> 00:13:57,614
Also control.

258
00:13:57,933 --> 00:14:03,126
Each new segment: four seconds of audio and video,
eight hundred twenty-three kilobytes,

259
00:14:03,126 --> 00:14:04,366
and it never changes.

260
00:14:04,646 --> 00:14:05,534
That is data.

261
00:14:05,866 --> 00:14:10,098
The player plays from its buffer, and switches
rendition when throughput changes.

262
00:14:13,533 --> 00:14:14,685
The master playlist.

263
00:14:15,033 --> 00:14:19,247
Three renditions: seven twenty p,
four eighty p, three sixty p.

264
00:14:19,527 --> 00:14:22,313
Each with a bandwidth, a resolution, and codecs.

265
00:14:22,633 --> 00:14:25,893
Bandwidth is video plus audio,
times one point one.

266
00:14:26,173 --> 00:14:29,846
Three thousand k plus one twenty-
eight k, times one point one,

267
00:14:29,846 --> 00:14:33,286
is three million four hundred forty
thousand eight hundred.

268
00:14:33,633 --> 00:14:37,681
It does not change during the match, so
fetch once and cache it for minutes.

269
00:14:37,961 --> 00:14:42,952
Some players re-poll it to switch CDNs, which
got standardised as Content Steering.

270
00:14:43,299 --> 00:14:49,473
And CODECS lets the player skip what it cannot
decode, say HEVC, and pick H.264.

271
00:14:49,753 --> 00:14:51,736
That is a fallback, not a retry.

272
00:14:52,016 --> 00:14:56,630
a v c one is H.264, m p four a is AAC.

273
00:14:57,566 --> 00:15:00,265
The media playlist slides forward like a window.

274
00:15:01,599 --> 00:15:07,110
At forty-seven seconds, the playlist has media
sequence one, and lists segments one to ten.

275
00:15:07,390 --> 00:15:11,618
Each with an EXTINF duration, and a
program date time: the wall clock.

276
00:15:11,966 --> 00:15:16,341
Four and a half seconds later: media
sequence two, segments two to eleven.

277
00:15:16,621 --> 00:15:18,018
The window slid by one.

278
00:15:18,366 --> 00:15:19,432
The reload rules.

279
00:15:19,712 --> 00:15:23,973
If the playlist changed, wait at least one
target duration before reloading.

280
00:15:24,299 --> 00:15:28,191
If it did not change, wait half a
target duration, then try again.

281
00:15:28,533 --> 00:15:31,913
And if the same sequence number now
points at a different URI,

282
00:15:31,913 --> 00:15:34,421
the spec says the player should halt playback.

283
00:15:34,766 --> 00:15:38,178
Video on demand is just a playlist
that stopped changing.

284
00:15:38,458 --> 00:15:42,866
It ends with EXT-X-ENDLIST, so the player
reads it once and stops polling.

285
00:15:43,799 --> 00:15:47,938
Open a segment, and it is a stream of
hundred and eighty-eight byte packets.

286
00:15:48,218 --> 00:15:49,583
MPEG transport stream.

287
00:15:49,933 --> 00:15:53,376
Four bytes of header, a hundred and
eighty-four bytes of payload.

288
00:15:53,699 --> 00:15:56,407
The header starts with a sync
byte, hex forty-seven.

289
00:15:56,687 --> 00:16:02,110
Then a thirteen-bit packet ID, which says whose
packet this is: audio, video, or tables.

290
00:16:02,390 --> 00:16:04,808
They share one byte stream: multiplexing.

291
00:16:05,088 --> 00:16:06,690
Then a continuity counter.

292
00:16:07,033 --> 00:16:10,664
Fixed length plus a sync byte: both
framing answers at once.

293
00:16:10,944 --> 00:16:13,616
And one eighty-eight was chosen for ATM.

294
00:16:14,633 --> 00:16:18,290
Seven twenty p: eight thousand five
hundred eighty-two packets,

295
00:16:18,290 --> 00:16:20,321
three point three percent overhead.

296
00:16:20,666 --> 00:16:23,156
Four eighty p: four point one percent.

297
00:16:23,499 --> 00:16:25,785
Three sixty p: five point six.

298
00:16:26,065 --> 00:16:32,167
The newer fragmented MP4, or CMAF, uses
boxes instead: size, type, payload.

299
00:16:32,447 --> 00:16:33,509
TLV again.

300
00:16:34,433 --> 00:16:37,467
How long should a segment be, with
fifty million viewers?

301
00:16:37,799 --> 00:16:42,536
Two-second segments: twenty-five million
segment requests a second at peak,

302
00:16:42,536 --> 00:16:45,756
and a latency floor of six
seconds, three segments.

303
00:16:46,099 --> 00:16:49,907
Three seconds: sixteen point seven
million, floor nine seconds.

304
00:16:50,233 --> 00:16:55,614
Four seconds, what our ffmpeg run used: twelve and
a half million, floor twelve seconds.

305
00:16:55,933 --> 00:17:00,601
Six seconds, Apple's advice: eight point
three million, floor eighteen seconds.

306
00:17:00,933 --> 00:17:05,538
Thirty seconds: one point seven million a
second, and ninety seconds behind.

307
00:17:05,818 --> 00:17:06,535
Too late.

308
00:17:06,866 --> 00:17:10,368
Shorter means more requests, more
keyframes, more round trips.

309
00:17:10,648 --> 00:17:14,796
Longer means more latency, and slower
reaction to bandwidth changes.

310
00:17:15,076 --> 00:17:19,915
A hybrid uses one to two second segments at
start-up, for a fast first frame.

311
00:17:20,833 --> 00:17:23,494
And the playlist is the harder traffic of the two.

312
00:17:24,533 --> 00:17:25,772
Tiny versus big.

313
00:17:26,099 --> 00:17:28,563
Changes every four seconds, versus never.

314
00:17:28,899 --> 00:17:32,650
A TTL of at most half a target
duration, versus forever.

315
00:17:32,999 --> 00:17:36,706
A stale playlist stalls the player or
points at deleted files.

316
00:17:36,986 --> 00:17:38,692
A stale segment is harmless.

317
00:17:39,033 --> 00:17:42,359
And playlists are billed by
requests, segments by bytes.

318
00:17:43,299 --> 00:17:45,297
The player's buffer is the stale cache.

319
00:17:45,633 --> 00:17:49,841
When the network has a bad minute on a train,
the buffer keeps the picture going.

320
00:17:50,166 --> 00:17:51,815
It drains while downloads stall.

321
00:17:52,166 --> 00:17:55,943
A large buffer survives a bad
minute, but costs latency,

322
00:17:55,943 --> 00:17:58,842
and memory on low-end phones and TV sticks.

323
00:17:59,166 --> 00:18:04,277
A one-segment buffer gives the lowest latency,
but one slow segment means a stall.

324
00:18:04,599 --> 00:18:07,281
More bandwidth means a smaller buffer is enough.

325
00:18:07,561 --> 00:18:10,434
When the playlist stops moving,
the buffer keeps playing.

326
00:18:11,366 --> 00:18:12,875
Latency is a budget.

327
00:18:13,155 --> 00:18:15,005
How far behind the stadium are you?

328
00:18:15,333 --> 00:18:17,136
Encoding: about two seconds.

329
00:18:17,466 --> 00:18:19,733
The segment has to fill: four seconds.

330
00:18:20,066 --> 00:18:22,773
Getting it to the CDN: about two seconds.

331
00:18:23,099 --> 00:18:26,870
And the player's hold-back: three
target durations, twelve seconds.

332
00:18:27,199 --> 00:18:30,910
About twenty seconds behind the
stadium, with four-second segments.

333
00:18:31,933 --> 00:18:35,553
Low-Latency HLS is four ideas from earlier weeks.

334
00:18:35,833 --> 00:18:38,133
Parts: one-second pieces of a segment.

335
00:18:38,466 --> 00:18:42,877
Blocking playlist reload: the request is
held until that segment exists.

336
00:18:43,157 --> 00:18:44,035
Long polling.

337
00:18:44,366 --> 00:18:46,448
Preload hints name the next part early.

338
00:18:46,728 --> 00:18:51,118
They replaced HTTP two server push,
in January twenty twenty.

339
00:18:51,466 --> 00:18:54,987
And delta updates: EXT-X-SKIP drops old entries.

340
00:18:55,267 --> 00:18:56,596
Diffs, for playlists.

341
00:18:56,876 --> 00:19:01,843
With one-second parts and a part hold-back of three
parts, you get to about three seconds.

342
00:19:02,766 --> 00:19:04,901
Now a problem only live sport has.

343
00:19:05,181 --> 00:19:05,964
The spoiler.

344
00:19:07,299 --> 00:19:09,106
The wicket falls at zero seconds.

345
00:19:09,433 --> 00:19:11,778
The scorecard shows it at about ten seconds.

346
00:19:12,099 --> 00:19:14,007
And the video shows it at about twenty.

347
00:19:14,333 --> 00:19:17,124
For ten seconds, the scorecard spoils the video.

348
00:19:17,466 --> 00:19:20,791
The fix: stamp each update with the
ball's wall-clock time.

349
00:19:21,071 --> 00:19:23,935
The player knows each segment's program date time,

350
00:19:23,935 --> 00:19:27,543
so it reveals the update only when
playback passes that moment.

351
00:19:28,466 --> 00:19:29,620
Adaptive bitrate.

352
00:19:29,900 --> 00:19:31,396
Who decides what is worse?

353
00:19:31,676 --> 00:19:35,311
The player, at every segment, under a
policy the product sets.

354
00:19:36,633 --> 00:19:39,411
The blue line is the throughput
the network gives you.

355
00:19:39,691 --> 00:19:42,031
It wobbles, and then dips, maybe a tunnel.

356
00:19:42,366 --> 00:19:46,014
The yellow steps are the rendition the
player picks for each segment.

357
00:19:46,294 --> 00:19:50,418
It steps down before the buffer runs dry,
and back up when things recover.

358
00:19:50,766 --> 00:19:54,704
Throughput-based ABR measures how
fast recent segments arrived,

359
00:19:54,704 --> 00:19:57,329
and picks the highest rendition that fits.

360
00:19:57,609 --> 00:19:59,772
Congestion control, one layer up.

361
00:20:00,099 --> 00:20:04,857
Buffer-based ABR steps down when the buffer is
nearly empty, and up when it is full.

362
00:20:05,137 --> 00:20:09,145
Netflix's AB tests showed ten to
twenty percent fewer rebuffers.

363
00:20:09,466 --> 00:20:12,755
And the policy: a stall is worse
than a lower rendition.

364
00:20:13,035 --> 00:20:17,104
Apple suggests starting cellular at
seven hundred thirty kilobits,

365
00:20:17,104 --> 00:20:18,706
and Wi-Fi at two megabits.

366
00:20:19,633 --> 00:20:23,765
At this scale, you use more than one
CDN, and more than one origin.

367
00:20:24,099 --> 00:20:30,121
The app talks to CDN A as primary: edge, mid-
tier, origin one with its own packager.

368
00:20:30,401 --> 00:20:33,394
CDN B is the fallback, with origin two.

369
00:20:33,674 --> 00:20:35,329
Same segment names on both.

370
00:20:36,366 --> 00:20:37,240
How to choose.

371
00:20:37,520 --> 00:20:43,101
DNS hands out a CDN per region, but
resolvers cache, so it is slow to change.

372
00:20:43,433 --> 00:20:46,943
List each rendition twice in the
master, once per CDN.

373
00:20:47,266 --> 00:20:53,084
Content Steering: a JSON file, polled
every TTL, names the preferred CDN.

374
00:20:53,433 --> 00:20:57,456
Or client failover: errors on one
CDN trip a circuit breaker,

375
00:20:57,456 --> 00:20:59,897
and the next segment tries the other.

376
00:21:00,177 --> 00:21:01,121
Session seven.

377
00:21:02,066 --> 00:21:03,492
Serve stale is your friend.

378
00:21:03,772 --> 00:21:05,541
With one catch, for live video.

379
00:21:05,866 --> 00:21:10,418
ffmpeg's delete segments option keeps
only one unreferenced segment.

380
00:21:10,698 --> 00:21:14,161
Segment zero vanished four seconds
after it left the playlist.

381
00:21:14,499 --> 00:21:18,894
Then the origin blips, and the CDN,
correctly, serves a stale playlist.

382
00:21:19,233 --> 00:21:23,067
But the stale playlist lists a segment
the origin already deleted.

383
00:21:23,399 --> 00:21:24,277
Four oh four.

384
00:21:24,557 --> 00:21:25,760
Negatively cached.

385
00:21:26,040 --> 00:21:27,083
The player stalls.

386
00:21:27,363 --> 00:21:29,347
Serve stale may cause player issues.

387
00:21:29,666 --> 00:21:35,827
The RFC says a removed segment must stay available
for its own duration plus the playlist's.

388
00:21:36,107 --> 00:21:38,346
Four plus forty: forty-four seconds.

389
00:21:38,666 --> 00:21:40,756
Fixes: raise the delete threshold.

390
00:21:41,036 --> 00:21:44,403
Keep the playlist TTL at most
half a target duration,

391
00:21:44,403 --> 00:21:48,151
and serve a stale playlist for one
target duration at most.

392
00:21:48,431 --> 00:21:51,350
Segments never change, so serve
those stale freely.

393
00:21:51,699 --> 00:21:52,422
Apple adds:

394
00:21:52,422 --> 00:21:55,704
treat a playlist as invalid when its Last-Modified

395
00:21:55,704 --> 00:21:59,118
is more than three target
durations behind the Date.

396
00:22:00,066 --> 00:22:02,859
Putting it together: caching
rules for a live stream.

397
00:22:03,899 --> 00:22:05,131
The master: minutes.

398
00:22:05,466 --> 00:22:09,925
The media playlist: at most half a target
duration, stale for one at most.

399
00:22:10,266 --> 00:22:12,266
Segments: long, they never change.

400
00:22:12,599 --> 00:22:15,415
And the scorecard: at most half
your freshness promise.

401
00:22:15,733 --> 00:22:17,700
Also, gzip every playlist.

402
00:22:17,980 --> 00:22:19,034
Apple says must.

403
00:22:19,314 --> 00:22:23,863
And keep per-viewer tokens out of the cache key,
or every viewer gets their own copy.

404
00:22:24,799 --> 00:22:27,875
Finally, making the stream yourself, with ffmpeg.

405
00:22:28,199 --> 00:22:29,083
A one-liner.

406
00:22:29,363 --> 00:22:33,561
Dash r e reads at native speed: a file
pretending to be a camera.

407
00:22:33,841 --> 00:22:35,900
Codec copy means no re-encode.

408
00:22:36,180 --> 00:22:39,423
Hls time four asks for four-second segments.

409
00:22:39,766 --> 00:22:43,220
What actually came out: eight point
three three second segments.

410
00:22:43,500 --> 00:22:48,824
Because with codec copy, ffmpeg can
only cut at existing keyframes,

411
00:22:48,824 --> 00:22:53,988
and x264's default keyframe interval
is two hundred fifty frames.

412
00:22:54,268 --> 00:22:56,892
Hls time is a target, not a promise.

413
00:22:57,233 --> 00:22:58,945
A real pipeline: camera,

414
00:22:59,266 --> 00:23:01,898
ingest over RTMP or SRT,

415
00:23:02,233 --> 00:23:03,099
transcoder,

416
00:23:03,433 --> 00:23:04,183
packager,

417
00:23:04,499 --> 00:23:05,225
origin,

418
00:23:05,566 --> 00:23:06,492
and CDN.

419
00:23:06,833 --> 00:23:09,157
The ABR ladder: split equals three.

420
00:23:09,437 --> 00:23:13,763
Decode once, encode three times: seven
twenty p at three thousand k,

421
00:23:13,763 --> 00:23:17,644
four eighty at fifteen hundred,
three sixty at eight hundred.

422
00:23:17,966 --> 00:23:22,518
A keyframe every hundred twenty frames,
with scene-cut keyframes disabled.

423
00:23:22,798 --> 00:23:26,122
That is four seconds, but only at
thirty frames per second.

424
00:23:26,402 --> 00:23:29,896
A twenty-four fps source gave
five-second segments.

425
00:23:30,233 --> 00:23:35,205
And the flags give a forty-second window that
never ends, stamped with wall-clock time.

426
00:23:36,133 --> 00:23:38,817
And that is the whole course,
in one cricket match.

427
00:23:39,866 --> 00:23:45,068
Week one, sockets: sixteen-bit ports, and base
sixty-four in the WebSocket handshake.

428
00:23:45,399 --> 00:23:47,699
Week two, the wire: framing everywhere.

429
00:23:47,979 --> 00:23:50,151
Blank lines, lengths, fixed packets.

430
00:23:50,499 --> 00:23:53,969
Week three, scale: fifty million held
sockets, and Little's Law.

431
00:23:54,299 --> 00:23:56,925
Week four, nginx: cache lock, use stale.

432
00:23:57,266 --> 00:24:02,301
Week five, HTTP: ETags, three oh
fours, the one oh one upgrade,

433
00:24:02,301 --> 00:24:04,858
and push's death shaping LL-HLS.

434
00:24:05,199 --> 00:24:10,276
Week six, CDNs: tiers, collapsing, negative
caching, and which bill you are on.

435
00:24:10,599 --> 00:24:15,618
Week seven, failures: jitter, the live-edge
four oh four, stale if error, failover.

436
00:24:15,966 --> 00:24:19,586
And week eight: HLS, a playlist
polled like a scorecard,

437
00:24:19,586 --> 00:24:21,912
pointing at bytes that never change.

438
00:24:22,833 --> 00:24:24,113
Four ideas to keep.

439
00:24:24,433 --> 00:24:24,935
One.

440
00:24:25,215 --> 00:24:26,693
Pull scales through caches.

441
00:24:26,973 --> 00:24:28,469
Push scales through machines.

442
00:24:28,749 --> 00:24:32,163
If the answer is the same for everyone, use a CDN.

443
00:24:32,443 --> 00:24:35,329
If it is different per person,
you are buying sockets.

444
00:24:35,666 --> 00:24:36,202
Two.

445
00:24:36,482 --> 00:24:38,814
Number the pieces, then cache them forever.

446
00:24:39,094 --> 00:24:42,246
A small changing index, plus
immutable numbered pieces.

447
00:24:42,566 --> 00:24:43,152
Three.

448
00:24:43,432 --> 00:24:45,997
Latency is a budget you spend on safety.

449
00:24:46,277 --> 00:24:50,471
Poll intervals, TTLs, segment length
and hold-back all add up.

450
00:24:50,799 --> 00:24:51,503
And four.

451
00:24:51,783 --> 00:24:53,130
Know which bill you are on.

452
00:24:53,410 --> 00:24:56,277
Below about eleven kilobytes you pay per request.

453
00:24:56,557 --> 00:24:57,694
Above it, per byte.

454
00:24:58,633 --> 00:25:01,989
Here is what the quiz is most likely
to ask from session eight.

455
00:25:37,733 --> 00:25:40,119
That is session eight, and the end of the course.

456
00:25:40,399 --> 00:25:45,873
From one echo server and seven system calls, to one
cricket match on fifty million phones.

457
00:25:46,153 --> 00:25:48,957
Every live stream is a
scorecard with bigger bytes.

458
00:25:49,237 --> 00:25:50,416
Good luck in the quiz.

